  "frustrations": "From a Magic: The Gathering player's perspective, having this kind of hobby is costly. While D&D is more accessible than MTG, a fully immersive experience includes (besides rulebooks) spending money on miniature figurines,  battle maps & 3D terrain pieces, character sheets & pencils etc. \nLong campaigns are hard to keep track of if your DM is not organized. \nAs a person who loves roleplaying and gets very serious when impersonating a fictional character, some D&D nights can lose some of the \"spark\" and things could get boring without props to keep the roleplaying part alive. Also, he hates getting out of character for doing stuff like computing complicated dice values/interactions or to search for rules in the books.",
  "scenarios": "The main attraction for him are the VR goggles, because they allow him to see his friends \"disguised\" as their favourite characters and see the magic \"come to life\" on the map. On the smartwatch, he enjoys gamified exploration features which helps him make decisions quickly without always keeping an eye on the phone's screen.",
  "otherDetails": "He cares less about the mathematical optimizations and logical aspects. His favourite part is the story, the relations between the characters. The most important aspect is to really feel like the \"Elf Archer\" that he plays.",
